Hecks.behavior_kind "back_aggregate_storage" do
  # The persistence PORT : back an aggregate's find / save / all with a
  # concrete store. SUBSTRATE, not a firing adapter — it has no trigger command
  # and chains into nothing ; it backs the repository the bus already uses.
  # :memory, :heki, :sqlite are DISTINCT adapter families off this one port,
  # each its own store. The adapter KIND is the choice, declared in the
  # consumer's hecksagon ; per-deployment connection config (sqlite db path,
  # postgres host) lives in the .world. (sqlite / postgres / mysql share the
  # SQL output and may nest as providers of a future :sql adapter — see i728.)
  #
  # Resolution differs from driving adapters : a driving adapter (e.g. the
  # report port) fires a kernel hook ON a command (trigger → hook → cascade).
  # This port is resolved at REPOSITORY-MINT time — the runtime picks the
  # backend constructor by adapter kind when it builds the aggregate's
  # repository. Growing the FrameworkRegistry a storage-backend hook category
  # to resolve it generically (instead of the hardcoded apply_sqlite_persistence
  # read of hex.persistence in rust/src/runtime/mod.rs) is i728 ; the panic
  # when :sqlite over-applies across a multi-aggregate domain is i735. This
  # contract is the source of truth for the declaration shape.

  vision "Back an aggregate's repository (find / save / all) with a concrete
          store. Substrate : no trigger, no response cascade — resolved at
          repository-mint time by adapter kind, not fired on a command.
          :memory / :heki / :sqlite are distinct adapter families off this
          port. Generic runtime resolution is i728 ; the over-apply panic is
          i735."

  # Substrate : no trigger command, no follow-on. Each adapter family off this
  # port supplies its own connection fields ; the store itself is the contract.
end
